From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] SPARC64: run the client interface with interrupts disabled

sparc64_of_client_interface saves the caller's register windows and
trap registers into a context (SAVE_CPU_STATE), resets the window
registers for the firmware and puts it all back on the way out
(RESTORE_CPU_STATE). Both macros walk %cwp through every window with
wrpr and rewrite %tl, %tpc, %tnpc, %tstate and %tba. They run with the
caller's PSTATE.IE. Solaris and illumos (client_handler in sun4u
mach_locore.S) call in with interrupts enabled and their own trap table
in %tba.

An interrupt taken in the middle of either walk runs the client's trap
handler with %cwp pointing at an arbitrary window while %cansave,
%canrestore and %otherwin still describe the caller's window. The
handler's save overwrites a window that holds the caller's registers,
and the trap registers it leaves behind are rewritten afterwards.
Tribblix m34 (illumos) on sun4u stopped in one of these ways in four of
seven boots of its live CD:

- client_handler's %l4 (the %pstate it restores after the call) comes
  back as 0: the kernel runs on in user mode at a kernel address, traps
  into a window spill loop at TL 1 (QEMU -d int: "Window Spill
  (v=00bc)" at priv_trap+0x44, endlessly);
- "panic[cpu0]/thread=180e000: bad stack overflow at TL 2" with trap
  frames from prom_finddevice whose %tpc are 0x1, 0x3 and 0x7.

With this change neither came back in about ten boots (live CD,
installs, installed systems).

Solaris 10 showed the same once in six boots of its DVD: "BAD TRAP:
type=4e" (interrupt level 14) with the PC in the firmware, from
prom_finddevice in vfs_mountroot.

Clear PSTATE.IE on entry, before the state is saved, keep the caller's
%pstate in the context so RESTORE_CPU_STATE puts the rest back with
interrupts still off, and write the caller's %pstate last, just before
returning. Clients that call in with interrupts disabled see no
change.
---
 arch/sparc64/call-client.S | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/sparc64/call-client.S b/arch/sparc64/call-client.S
index 4129505..2b0cd3b 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/call-client.S
+++ b/arch/sparc64/call-client.S
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include "cpustate.h"
+#include "pstate.h"
 
 	.globl	sparc64_of_client_interface, client_tba
 
@@ -44,6 +45,18 @@ sparc64_of_client_interface:
 	stx	%g6, [%sp + 2047 - 248 + 232]
 	stx	%g7, [%sp + 2047 - 248 + 240]
 
+	/*
+	 * No interrupts until the client's state is back: SAVE_CPU_STATE and
+	 * RESTORE_CPU_STATE rotate %cwp through every window and rewrite the
+	 * trap registers, and a client whose trap table takes an interrupt in
+	 * between (Solaris and illumos call in with PSTATE.IE set) gets its
+	 * register windows and trap state overwritten. %g2 keeps the client's
+	 * %pstate.
+	 */
+	rdpr	%pstate, %g2
+	andn	%g2, PSTATE_IE, %g3
+	wrpr	%g3, %pstate
+
 	/* Save existing stack */
 	setx	client_stack, %g6, %g7
 	stx	%sp, [%g7]
@@ -77,6 +90,9 @@ sparc64_of_client_interface:
 	
 	SAVE_CPU_STATE(cif)
 	
+	/* The client's %pstate as it called in (SAVE_CPU_STATE saw IE cleared) */
+	stx	%g2, [%g1 + 0xb0]
+
 	RESET_CPU_WINDOW_STATE(cif)
 	
 	/* Update __context to point to saved area */
@@ -117,6 +133,11 @@ sparc64_of_client_interface:
 	setx	__context, %g6, %g7
 	stx	%g3, [%g7]
 	
+	/* The client's state back with interrupts still off, its %pstate (%g2) last */
+	ldx	[%g1 + 0xb0], %g2
+	andn	%g2, PSTATE_IE, %g3
+	stx	%g3, [%g1 + 0xb0]
+
 	RESTORE_CPU_STATE(cif)
 	
 	add	%g1, CONTEXT_STATE_SIZE, %g5
@@ -130,6 +151,9 @@ sparc64_of_client_interface:
 	/* Restore %pc */
 	ldx	[%g1 + 0x4d0], %o7
 	
+	/* Interrupts as the client had them */
+	wrpr	%g2, %pstate
+
 	/* Restore globals */
 	ldx	[%g1 + 0x38], %g2
 	ldx	[%g1 + 0x40], %g3
